---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Best Treadmills Under $400 (2026): 6 Folding Picks With Incline";
const description =
  "Six folding treadmills selling for $340 to $380 on Amazon on 26 Nov 2026, with up to 18% auto incline, 250 to 400 lb ratings and 42 to 51-inch belts.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "merach-18-auto-incline",
    name: "MERACH 18% Auto Incline Treadmill",
    brand: "MERACH",
    category: "Best Treadmill Under $400 Overall",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FVT465SQ?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41ndMxgNscL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $380 (Amazon, 26 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":380,"high":380},
    specs: "18 x 47 in. belt · up to 8.7 mph · 18% auto incline · 350 lb capacity · 122 lb machine weight · hydraulic fold (per the Amazon listing)",
    pros: [
      "The steepest powered incline on this page, at 18% per the listing",
      "A 350 lb rating backed by a 2.0 mm core-steel frame and a 122 lb machine weight",
      "An 18-inch-wide belt with a multi-layer shock-absorbing system",
      "Speed and incline adjustable from a remote panel or the MERACH app",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The listing covers more than one configuration (15/18% incline, 47 or 55-inch belt); check which one is selected at this price",
      "A 47-inch belt is better for walking and jogging than running",
      "No warranty term stated in the listing; it promises 24/7 support only",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The best balance of incline, capacity and belt size we found under $400, provided the configuration you select is the 18%, 47-inch model the bullets describe.",
    description: "MERACH's 18% auto incline treadmill is the pick for most people because it does the most of what this price band can do well. The Amazon listing describes an 18% powered incline, an 18 by 47-inch non-slip belt, a top speed of 8.7 mph and a 350 lb weight capacity, with a frame of 2.0 mm core steel that brings the machine's own weight to 122 lb. A heavy frame is a better sign of capacity than a large number on a box, and among these six only the WELLFIT claims more. The listing also describes a multi-layer shock-absorbing system, an LED display showing speed, time, distance, calories, incline and heart rate, adjustment from a remote panel or the MERACH app, operation under 45 decibels and hydraulic folding in about 30 seconds. The catch is the title. It reads \"15/18% Auto Incline, 55\"/47\"x18\"/19\" Wide Belt, 0.5-8.7MPH/10MPH\", which means the page covers two versions, and other MERACH listings for this machine were priced at $509.99 and $552.49 on the same day. The bullets describe the 18%, 47-inch version; confirm that is the one selected before you pay. Amazon showed $380.12 on 26 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "goplus-4-75hp-15-incline",
    name: "Goplus 4.75HP Folding Treadmill with 15% Auto Incline",
    brand: "Goplus",
    category: "Best for Jogging and Taller Users",
    badge: "Longest Belt",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Goplus%204.75HP%20Folding%20Treadmill%20with%2015%25%20Auto%20Incline+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $340 (Amazon, 26 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":340,"high":340},
    specs: "51 x 18 in. running area · 0.6–10.8 mph · 0–15% auto incline · 20 programs · hydraulic fold · capacity not stated (per the Amazon listing)",
    pros: [
      "The longest running area here, 51 by 18 inches per the listing",
      "The highest top speed here, 10.8 mph, with 15% auto incline",
      "Control by touch screen, the Gymax app, or a detachable knob that works as a remote with voice control",
      "Extra-long handrails with heart rate sensors, and a six-point cushioning system",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The listing does not state a maximum user weight",
      "No warranty term stated in the listing",
      "The 4.75 HP figure is not a continuous rating and should not be compared as one",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The most running-friendly machine under $400 on paper. Ask Goplus for the weight limit before you buy if you are over about 250 lb.",
    description: "The Goplus 4.75HP is the machine on this page for anyone who wants to jog regularly or is tall. Its listing describes a 51 by 18-inch running area, the longest and among the widest here, with a five-layer belt and a six-point buffer system, plus a speed range of 0.6 to 10.8 mph and a 0 to 15% automatic incline. Twenty preset programs, dual Bluetooth speakers, a device holder and an LED touch screen come with it, and the machine can also be controlled through Goplus's Gymax app or a removable control knob that doubles as a remote and accepts voice commands. The listing describes a steel frame, folding with a hydraulic rod you press with a foot, transport wheels and extra-long handrails with heart rate sensors. Two things are missing, and both matter. The listing does not state a maximum user weight, which is unusual at this price and the first thing to ask about if you are a heavier user. Nor does it state a warranty term. Goplus also sells the SuperFit 2-in-1 pads covered elsewhere on this site. Amazon showed this model at $339.99 on 26 November 2026, the same price on two separate listings.",
  },
  {
    id: "wellfit-4hp-400lb",
    name: "WELLFIT 4.0HP Heavy Duty Foldable Home Treadmill",
    brand: "WELLFIT",
    category: "Best Under $400 for Heavier Users",
    badge: "Highest Capacity",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=WELLFIT%204.0HP%20Heavy%20Duty%20Foldable%20Home%20Treadmill+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $380 (Amazon, 26 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":380,"high":380},
    specs: "18 x 43 in. belt · 0.6–8.8 mph · 15-level auto incline · 400 lb capacity · folds to 31.89 x 28.74 x 44.1 in. (per the Amazon listing)",
    pros: [
      "A 400 lb weight rating, the highest on this page",
      "The only listing here with a multi-year term: 3 years on the frame, 1 year on parts and motor",
      "An 18-inch-wide belt, a soft-drop hydraulic fold and 95% pre-installed assembly",
      "Incline adjustable from the console or by voice command",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The listing gives 15 incline levels but not the top percentage",
      "A 43-inch belt is short for running",
      "Needs a grounded outlet, which the listing flags as a warning",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The pick for heavier users who mainly walk, with the clearest warranty terms on this page. Ask WELLFIT for the maximum incline grade if it matters to you.",
    description: "WELLFIT's 4.0HP home treadmill is here for its weight rating. The listing claims a 400 lb capacity on a reinforced alloy steel frame, the highest figure among these six, and pairs it with an 18 by 43-inch multi-layer belt, speeds from 0.6 to 8.8 mph and 15 levels of automatic incline adjustable from the console or by voice command. It describes operation under 45 decibels, a soft-drop hydraulic fold to 31.89 by 28.74 by 44.1 inches, arrival 95% pre-installed with setup in about 10 minutes, a display showing heart rate, incline and calories, built-in speakers and an emergency-stop safety key. It is also the only listing here to state a multi-year term: a 3-year guarantee on the frame and 1 year on parts and motor, with US-based technical support. The gaps are the incline grade, given only as levels, and the belt length, which suits walking better than running. The listing warns that it must be plugged into a grounded outlet. It is a different listing from the WELLFIT Heavy Duty Auto Incline Foldable Treadmill in our under-$500 guide. Amazon showed $379.96 on 26 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "xterra-trx1000",
    name: "Xterra Fitness TRX1000 Compact Series Treadmill",
    brand: "Xterra",
    category: "Best Brand-Name Treadmill Under $400",
    badge: "Best-Known Brand",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FBKNYX52?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/418VaHJC4zL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $370 (Amazon, 26 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":370,"high":370},
    specs: "16 x 50 in. running surface · 2.25 HP · 0.5–10 mph (XTERRA) · 3 manual incline levels (XTERRA) · 250 lb capacity (XTERRA) · folds",
    pros: [
      "A 16 by 50-inch running surface, the second-longest here",
      "Up to 10 mph and 12 workout programs, per XTERRA's product page",
      "From a long-established treadmill brand with US-based support",
      "About $80 less on Amazon than XTERRA's own sale price on the same day",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Incline is manual, three positions set at the rear, not powered",
      "A 250 lb limit, the lowest here",
      "The Amazon listing itself gives few figures; most come from XTERRA's site",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The machine for buyers who would rather have a familiar brand and a 50-inch deck than a powered incline. Check your weight against the 250 lb limit.",
    description: "The XTERRA TRX1000 is the one machine here from a brand most people will recognise from sporting goods stores, and it trades features for that. The Amazon listing is short: a 2.25 HP motor, a 16 by 50-inch walking and running surface, a 5-inch LCD, a heavy-duty steel frame and console crossbar, and a folding deck. XTERRA's own product page, read on 26 November 2026, fills in the rest: a 250 lb maximum user weight, 0.5 to 10 mph, three levels of manual incline, 12 workout programs, hand-pulse sensors, a fold with Lift Assist, and set-up dimensions of 64 by 29.5 by 51 inches. The 50-inch surface is the main reason to choose it over the cheaper machines here, and 10 mph is enough for a run. The incline is the main reason not to: it is set by hand at the rear support, so it suits a fixed setting rather than intervals. XTERRA's store listed the TRX1000 at $449.99 (regular $599.99) that day, while Amazon showed $370.17, which is a large gap; check the seller on the Amazon listing before you buy. XTERRA's brand page on this site covers the TRX1200 and TRX1400 above it.",
  },
  {
    id: "ateedge-15-auto-incline-foldable",
    name: "ATEEDGE 15% Auto Incline Foldable Treadmill",
    brand: "ATEEDGE",
    category: "Best Under $400 for Programmed Workouts",
    badge: "Most Programs",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=ATEEDGE%2015%25%20Auto%20Incline%20Foldable%20Treadmill+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $369 (Amazon, 26 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":369,"high":369},
    specs: "18 x 42.5 in. belt · 0.5–10 mph · 15% auto incline (15 levels) · 350 lb capacity · 36 programs · folds to 35.43 x 27.5 x 48.43 in. (per the Amazon listing)",
    pros: [
      "36 preset programs, the most here, with 15 incline steps to 15%",
      "A 350 lb rating and an 18-inch-wide belt",
      "Double-layer deck with 8 internal and 6 external cushions, per the listing",
      "Fixed and removable device holders, Bluetooth app and speakers",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 42.5-inch belt is short for the 10 mph top speed",
      "Motor described only as \"3-peak HP\"",
      "No warranty term stated in the listing",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For walkers who like a program to vary incline and speed for them, with a 350 lb rating. Not the one for long running strides.",
    description: "The ATEEDGE is the pick for people who would rather follow a program than set speed and incline themselves. Its listing describes 36 preset programs, 15 incline options up to a 15% maximum and a speed range of 0.5 to 10 mph, enough for interval walking and short running efforts. It quotes an 18 by 42.5-inch belt on a double-layer deck with 8 built-in and 6 external cushions, a 350 lb capacity, a fixed and a removable device holder, a Bluetooth connection to an app and built-in speakers. It is one of only two listings here to give both set-up and folded dimensions: 54.33 by 27.5 by 48.43 inches open and 35.43 by 27.5 by 48.43 inches folded. The motor is described as \"3-peak HP\", a peak figure, and no warranty term is stated. The belt is the limitation: at 42.5 inches it is a walking and jogging length, and the 10 mph top speed is more than its deck is suited to for most adults. A related ATEEDGE listing with a 20% incline and 45.5-inch belt was also around $370 that day, but does not describe a folding deck, so it is not included here. Amazon showed $369.48 on 26 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "merach-t12",
    name: "Merach T12 Foldable Treadmill",
    brand: "Merach",
    category: "Best Under $400 for Small Rooms",
    badge: "Most Compact Fold",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F1L2BF59?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41u7e+DuBBL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $340 (Amazon, 26 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":340,"high":340},
    specs: "42.5 x 16.5 in. belt · 0.5–7.5 mph · 15% auto incline · 300 lb capacity · folds to 49.2 x 25.6 x 27.6 in. · 1-year warranty (per the Amazon listing)",
    pros: [
      "Folds to 27.6 inches tall, the lowest folded height here",
      "15% auto incline and a 300 lb rating at the bottom of this price band",
      "Under 40 dB by the listing, with heart rate sensors and 12 programs",
      "A stated 1-year warranty and 90% pre-assembly",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 7.5 mph top speed and 42.5-inch belt make it a walking and light-jogging machine",
      "The title quotes \"0-6/15%\" incline; check that the configuration selected is the 15% one",
      "Narrower belt (16.5 in.) than four of the other five",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The machine for a small apartment where storage matters more than stride length, with incline and a 300 lb rating for about $340.",
    description: "The Merach T12 is the smallest machine here when folded, which makes it the pick for flats and spare rooms. Its listing gives folded dimensions of 49.2 by 25.6 by 27.6 inches, so it stores lower and flatter than the upright folds of the others, with wheels for moving it. Set up, it offers a 42.5 by 16.5-inch belt, 0.5 to 7.5 mph, a 15% automatic incline and a 300 lb maximum user weight, on a double deck with four silicone cushions and six shock-absorbing pillars. The listing quotes a 2.5 HP motor, operation under 40 decibels, built-in heart rate sensors, 12 preset programs and the MERACH app, and it is one of only two listings here to state a warranty: one year, with lifetime customer support. It arrives 90% pre-assembled. The limits follow from its size: 7.5 mph and a 42.5-inch belt make it a walking machine that will take a gentle jog. Its title quotes incline as \"0-6/15%\", which suggests a second configuration with a 6% maximum; the bullets describe 15%, so check the selection before you buy. MERACH sells a T16 at $379.96 with a slightly longer 43.5-inch belt if you want a little more room. Amazon showed the T12 at $339.99 on 26 November 2026.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "By Price", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/price/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Treadmills Under $400", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-treadmills-under-400/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you get a good treadmill for under $400?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, for walking, incline walking and jogging. Between $300 and $400 you can buy a real folding treadmill with handrails, a console and, usually, a powered incline of up to 15 to 18%. What this budget does not buy is a 55 to 60-inch running deck with incline, or a long motor and parts warranty.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best folding treadmill with incline under $400?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On 26 November 2026 our pick was the MERACH 18% Auto Incline Treadmill at about $380, with an 18% incline, an 18 by 47-inch belt and a 350 lb rating per its listing. The Goplus 4.75HP at about $340 has a longer 51-inch running area and 15% incline, but its listing does not state a weight limit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is there a 300 lb folding treadmill with incline under $400?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Four of the six here state a rating of 300 lb or more with a powered incline: the WELLFIT 4.0HP (400 lb), the MERACH 18% model and the ATEEDGE (350 lb each) and the Merach T12 (300 lb). For a 300 lb user, pick one rated at least 50 lb higher, which points to the WELLFIT, MERACH or ATEEDGE.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run on a $400 treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You can jog, and on the longer-belt machines you can run for short sessions. The Goplus (51 inches) and XTERRA TRX1000 (50 inches) are the most suitable for running here, with top speeds of 10.8 and 10 mph. Regular running is better served by a 55 to 60-inch deck, which generally costs more than $400.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will treadmills under $400 be cheaper on Black Friday?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Some will and some will not. Prices on budget treadmills often move during Black Friday week, in both directions. Compare the price you see with the 26 November prices on this page, and be wary of a new \"was\" price that is far above anything the machine has recently sold for.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I buy a walking pad or a folding treadmill for $400?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If you want handrails, a console, a powered incline or a belt longer than about 45 inches, buy a folding treadmill. If the machine has to slide under a bed or a desk, buy a walking pad, which costs less for the same walking performance. At $400 you can afford either, so the choice comes down to space and how you plan to use it.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-26",
      dateModified: "2026-11-26",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 6,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-treadmills-under-400/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/best-treadmills-under-400/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "price");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-26",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-26",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best treadmill under 400",
      "treadmill under 400",
      "folding treadmill with incline",
      "treadmill 300 lb folding incline",
      "cheap incline treadmill",
      "budget folding treadmill",
      "xterra trx1000",
      "merach t12",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Best Overall: MERACH 18% Auto Incline Treadmill", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/price/">By Price</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buying Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Best Treadmills Under $400</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Between $300 and $400 you stop buying walking pads and start buying real folding treadmills: a deck with handrails, a console and, on five of these six, a powered incline. The MERACH 18% Auto Incline Treadmill is the pick for most people, the Goplus 4.75HP has the longest belt and highest speed, and the WELLFIT 4.0HP carries the highest weight rating.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 26, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">How we chose: makers’ published specifications and manuals, independent reviews and owner reports, cross-checked. Not tested hands-on.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We may earn a commission when you buy through the links below. It costs you nothing and never decides a ranking. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#merach-18-auto-incline"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41ndMxgNscL._SL500_.jpg" alt="MERACH 18% Auto Incline Treadmill" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#xterra-trx1000"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/418VaHJC4zL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Xterra Fitness TRX1000 Compact Series Treadmill" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#merach-t12"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41u7e+DuBBL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Merach T12 Foldable Treadmill" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>MERACH 18% Auto Incline Treadmill · Xterra Fitness TRX1000 Compact Series Treadmill · Merach T12 Foldable Treadmill</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          $400 is the price at which a home treadmill stops being a walking pad with ambitions.
          Every machine on this page has an upright console, handrails and a deck that folds up on
          a hinge, and five of the six raise the deck with a motor. What this budget still does not
          buy is a long running deck, a published continuous-duty motor rating or a long parts
          warranty. Below are six machines that were selling for $340 to $380 on Amazon on
          26 November 2026, what each listing actually states, and where the figures run out.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Overall:</span> <a href="#merach-18-auto-incline">MERACH 18% Auto Incline Treadmill</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Longest Belt:</span> <a href="#goplus-4-75hp-15-incline">Goplus 4.75HP Folding Treadmill with 15% Auto Incline</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Highest Capacity:</span> <a href="#wellfit-4hp-400lb">WELLFIT 4.0HP Heavy Duty Foldable Home Treadmill</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best-Known Brand:</span> <a href="#xterra-trx1000">Xterra Fitness TRX1000 Compact Series Treadmill</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
