        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Can you get a good treadmill for under $400?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, for walking, incline walking and jogging. Between $300 and $400 you can buy a real folding treadmill with handrails, a console and, usually, a powered incline of up to 15 to 18%. What this budget does not buy is a 55 to 60-inch running deck with incline, or a long motor and parts warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best folding treadmill with incline under $400?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On 26 November 2026 our pick was the MERACH 18% Auto Incline Treadmill at about $380, with an 18% incline, an 18 by 47-inch belt and a 350 lb rating per its listing. The Goplus 4.75HP at about $340 has a longer 51-inch running area and 15% incline, but its listing does not state a weight limit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is there a 300 lb folding treadmill with incline under $400?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Four of the six here state a rating of 300 lb or more with a powered incline: the WELLFIT 4.0HP (400 lb), the MERACH 18% model and the ATEEDGE (350 lb each) and the Merach T12 (300 lb). For a 300 lb user, pick one rated at least 50 lb higher, which points to the WELLFIT, MERACH or ATEEDGE.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you run on a $400 treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              You can jog, and on the longer-belt machines you can run for short sessions. The Goplus (51 inches) and XTERRA TRX1000 (50 inches) are the most suitable for running here, with top speeds of 10.8 and 10 mph. Regular running is better served by a 55 to 60-inch deck, which generally costs more than $400.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Will treadmills under $400 be cheaper on Black Friday?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some will and some will not. Prices on budget treadmills often move during Black Friday week, in both directions. Compare the price you see with the 26 November prices on this page, and be wary of a new "was" price that is far above anything the machine has recently sold for.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I buy a walking pad or a folding treadmill for $400?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              If you want handrails, a console, a powered incline or a belt longer than about 45 inches, buy a folding treadmill. If the machine has to slide under a bed or a desk, buy a walking pad, which costs less for the same walking performance. At $400 you can afford either, so the choice comes down to space and how you plan to use it.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

